{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for 9640b02a (M1 host fetch ownership and the object format).\n\nThe host owns the fetch. In M1 the hosting deployment resolves the install URI itself over smart HTTP: it fetches exactly the named commit, walks only the bounded objects needed to reach the named path, reads that path as a regular blob under the byte bound, and admits the install only when those bytes produce the expected release id and that id and the publisher key are on the operator's trust list. A CLI upload is not a substitute, as you say: it would prove the release and the publisher but not that the URI names those bytes. No subprocess, no fallback.\n\nSHA-256 is an explicit refusal in M1, not a second transport. The URI grammar keeps both lengths, 40 or 64 hex, unchanged, so a later SHA-256 transport changes no URI and no act form. When the host receives a 64-hex commit it refuses the install before any network call, with reason unsupported-object-format, bound to the install act and recorded in the rules scope as a refused install like any other, with the URI as presented. No partial fetch, no retry in another format, no silent downgrade. The Node CLI may resolve both formats with native Git for local checks; the install half's implementation note says that the CLI's acceptance of a SHA-256 release is not host admission, and names the host's SHA-256 smart-HTTP transport as a gap at its boundary. That transport is not owed in M1 and is not a plan 033 item; it is a later slice of its own. Nothing here changes the install act's two fields, the trust list, the one recorded install, the exact regular blob and byte bound, or historical replay."}